.TH LINK.D 4 "31 October 2026" "amix-base"
.SH NAME
link.d \- the network drivers present, one file each
.SH SYNOPSIS
.BI /etc/inet/link.d/ driver
.SH DESCRIPTION
Each network driver package puts one file in
.BR /etc/inet/link.d ,
named for the driver's kernel module, that tells
.BR ifup (1M)
how to find the driver's interfaces.
The file is the driver's own data, replaced with the package; it is not
edited.
How each interface is set up is in
.BR /etc/inet/interfaces ,
not here.
.PP
The file holds one line (a
.B #
starts a comment):
.PP
.nf
.IB "prefix  node  first  major" " [" "presence command ..." ]
.fi
.TP
.I prefix
the interface name before the unit number, as IP knows it
.RB ( aen
for
.BR aen0 ).
.TP
.I node
the device node before the unit number
.RB ( /dev/aen
for
.BR /dev/aen0 ).
.TP
.I first
the first unit number, usually 0.
.TP
.I major
the node's major number, used to make a missing node when the running
kernel's registry does not name the module.
.TP
.I presence command
optional: a command that fails when no such card is fitted; the driver's
units are not probed then.
.PP
.B ifup
opens the units one after another, from
.IR first ,
and stops at the first that does not open.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
.ft CW
aen     /dev/aen    0   18   /usr/amiga/bin/aen -S
.ft R
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ifup (1M),
.BR interfaces (4)
